+# FAQ for newcomers
+
+## How do I install the command line tool?
+
+With pipx (needs Python 3.11 or newer): `pipx install tally-toolkit`. Check it with `tallyctl version`. If the command is not found, run `pipx ensurepath` and open a new terminal.
+
+## What is the tool called now?
+
+`tallyctl`. It was `tally-cli` until the 2026-09 release; the old name works as an alias for one more release and is then removed.
+
+## Which port does tally listen on?
+
+8731. The old port 8080 is closed, so anything still using it gets a refused connection.
+
+## How do I clear the cache?
+
+`tallyctl cache clear` clears all regions; add `--region NAME` to clear just one. The old purge command was removed.
+
+## How long until an unanswered page escalates?
+
+15 minutes without acknowledgement. It goes from the primary to the secondary on-call, then to the engineering manager.
+
+## Does the pager wake me at night?
+
+Only for severity 1: customer-facing data loss or a full outage. Between 22:00 and 07:00, severity 2 incidents do not page; the primary picks them up in the morning.
+
+## What do I do when the ingest queue is stuck?
+
+Restart ingest with a drain first: `tallyctl restart ingest --drain`. It finishes in-flight work, parks the stuck batch in the dead-letter table and starts the consumer clean. A plain restart just picks up the same bad batch. Look for the cause afterwards.
+
+## What do I do when the API certificate has expired?
+
+Confirm the expiry with `tallyctl certs list`, renew with `tallyctl certs renew api`, then restart with `tallyctl restart api`. The new certificate is only served after the restart.
